From b05361dfcb432eb9344566cc69d9cd9d73d06c7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Huy3ko <hugundlach@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 19:28:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(belief): staleness decay depends on elapsed time, not on
 call count
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The two ignored regression tests in engine/tests/belief_decay_semantics.rs
carried the measurement: a second revision at the SAME instant decayed again
(0.1000 -> 0.0050), and 30 revisions one day apart destroyed a belief that one
revision after 30 days left at 0.1000. `apply_staleness_decay` multiplied the
running value by a factor recomputed from the same evidence timestamp on every
call, so a maintenance loop that runs once per turn erased every conviction.

The value is now recomputed from the base the evidence supports
(`BeliefPayload.undecayed_log_odds`, `#[serde(default)]` for existing rows),
never from what the previous pass wrote:

* idempotent — two passes at the same instant produce the same value;
* call-count independent — only elapsed time since the newest evidence counts,
  whatever the loop granularity;
* persistence-weighted — `attrs.persistence` resists TIME decay
  (rate * (1 - persistence); 1.0 never decays) but does not shield against
  contradicting evidence, which is assertion, not revision.

The base moves with every change to log-odds (`shift_log_odds` / `set_log_odds`:
evidence, propagated contribution, confirm, refute), so a re-assertion cannot
promote an already-decayed value to "undecayed" and charge the same elapsed
time twice. Found in the two-process acceptance run, pinned by a unit test that
fails against the naive variant (1.4184 -> 1.1177).

Tests: both ignored tests re-enabled and green (1.5760 -> 1.5760;
1.3360 == 1.3360), plus persistence weighting against the documented formula,
persistence 1.0 vs. contradiction, the re-assertion case, and a DB round-trip
proving that both attrs.persistence and the payload anchor survive persist/load.
